-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ROUTING-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Router
	> Layer 3 device
	> Separate IP address on each interface connected to a network
	> 2 functions:
		1) Prepare routing table/Determine best path to available networks - Through Routing Protocol + Statically configured routes by Admin
		2) Forward traffic to networks according to routing table - Automatic

Routing Table
	> Contains best route(s) to each known network+prefix

Route
	> Format: DestinationNetworkIP DestinationNetworkMask NextHopRouterIP
	> Types:
		(For networks directly connected to the router:)
		1) Connected
			> Route to a directly connected network
			> Automatically added to routing table when an ip address is configured on an interface of the router
		2) Local
			> Accompanies a connected route, automatically added when a connected route is added to routing table
			> Has a /32 mask, only to shows the ip address configured on the interface
		(For destinations not directly connected to the router, routes stating the nextHopRouterIP address are required:)
		3) Static
			> Statically configured by an administrator
			> If no dynamic routes, then all routers in topology should have static routes to all networks, for end-to-end connectivity
			> Summarization:
				> Multiple static routes having the same nextHopIP can be combined to a single static route with a broader network mask
				> Reduces memory usage on routers
			> Longest Prefix Match:
				> In a packet transfer, if multiple static routes are matched, one with more precise mask is used
			> Load Balancing:
				> In a packet transfer, if multiple static routes with equal precise mask are matched, then traffic will be load balanced across matched routes
				> In load balancing, traffic for a signle flow will always go over the same path, to avoid packets reaching out of order
			> Default Route/Gateway of Last Resort
				> A static route to which all unmatched traffic matches, eg. ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 203.0.113.2
				> Least prefered since least precise subnet mask, so catches only the unmatched traffic 
		4) Dynamic
			> Dynamically learned through a routing protocol
				> Routers automatically advertise their best path for known networks to each other
				> Routers then use this information to determine their own best paths to the known networks => Routing table is prepared
				> If the network changes, routers update each other, calculate new best path, and update their routing table
			> Summary routes are automatically used, to save memory, and cpu cycles spent in calculating a new path if a link goes down
			> Types (Breif):
				1) IGPs (Interior Gateway Protocols) - for routing within organisation
					1) Distance Vector Routing Protocol:
						> Routers share routing tables with immediate neighbours only => Routers have only limited/localised view
						> Each router sends its directly connected neighbours a list of all its known networks along with its own distance to each network
						> FASTER but LESS ACCURATE
						1) RIP (Routing Information Protocol)
							Easiest, but low scalability
						2) EIGRP (Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing Protocol)
							Easy & quick, but Cisco propreitry
					2) Link State Routing Protocol:
						> Floods the "state" of all links to all other links in topology to build a consistent network map across all routers
						> Each link shares info to its directly connected links => Info is passed on unchnged => Each link learns the actual network topology
						> SLOWER but MORE ACCURATE
						1) OSPF (Open Shortest Path First)
							Open source, most common
						2) IS-IS (Intermediate System - Intermediate System)
							Used in service providers and MPLS networks
				2) EGPs (Exterior Gateway Protocols) - for routing outside organisation, over internet
					> Path Vector
						1) BGP (Border Gateway Protocol)
			> Metric:
				> In routing protocols, each path from origin to destination is assigned a metric value based a certain attribute (hops or bandwidth) of that path
				> Different IGPs calculate the metric in different way
				> Each router gets advertised with metrics of paths to all accessible networks in the topology by its directly connected neighbours
				> For each advertised route, it adds the corresponding metric b/w the advertisor router and itself to the advertised route to build the final metric
				> Then, for each destination, the route with the lowest metric makes it into the routing table for the router
				> Other, non-best routes, go into their respective rouitng protocol db
				> If best path to a destination goes down, it'll be removed from routing table, routes to that destination across all DBs will be compared, 
				1) RIP - Hop Count (No. of routers on way from origin to destination)
					> For a destination, the path with least hops is most preffered
					> Max. hops = 15, destinations with >15 hops are marked unreachable => RIP feasible only in test/small environments
					> Each router advertises, to its directly connected neighbours, the no. of hops each network in topology is away from it
					> A router upon recieving this info, adds 1 hop to each route's metric, to calculate the final metrics for all recieved routes
					> For each destination, the route with the lowest hop count makes it into the routing table
				2) OSPF - Cost (for all routers in path from origin to destination, SUM(Reference Bandwidth/Exit Interface Bandwidth))
					> Manually configuring interface bandwidth => manupulating link cost => manupulating path
				3) IS-IS - Cost (By default, cost is derived from hop count, not from interface bandwidth, can be explicitly configured)
				4) EIGRP - Cost (Derived from interface bandwidth and delay of links)
				> Load Balancing:
					> Multi routes to same destintion have eq metrics, all routes make it to routing table, router load-balances outbound traffic to destination
					> Only EIGRP is capable of Unequal Cost Multi Path Load Balancing
			> Administrative Distance:
				> If paths to same destination are recieved from different routing protocols, their metrics cannot be compared
				> AD is used to compare routes recieved from different protocols
				> Each protocol has an AD (preferrence over other protocols)
				> Lower AD is preferred
				> AD can be manually changed for a route
				> Default AD:
					1) Connected Routes - 0
					2) Static Routes - 1
					3) BGP - 20
					4) EIGRP - 90
					5) OSPF - 110
					6) IS-IS - 115
					7) RIP - 120
			> Route Precedence:
				> If router running multiple routing protocols learns multiple routes to same NETWORK+PREFIX, deciding order:
					1st) Least AD
					2nd) Least metric
				  For each NETWORK+PREFIX, best route put in routing table (if multi. best, all put in table for LB), others put in respective routing DBs as backups
				> Hence, routing table stores best route(s) for each NETWORK+PREFIX
				  In packet forwarding, if destiantion IP mathces multiple NETWORK+PREFIX in routing table, route with longest/most specific prefix is used
			> Floating Static Routes:
				> Static routes with manually configured AD, as static backup routes (manual AD >=0)
			> Loopback Interfaces:
				> Best practice to assign 1 loopback interface to router (for ID) (single is used for multiple puposes, multiple possible but not required)
				> Logical Interfaces => Cant go down
				> Logical Interfaces => Cannot physically be in same network as other devices => /32 subnet mask
				> Used for traffic terminating on the router itself (management traffic, VoIP, BGP peering) & as Router ID in OSPF
				> Access router via lb int, for router management (since physical int could go down anytime, loopback can never) => router always accessible
				> This provides redunduncy if there are multiple routes to the router
			> Passive Interfaces:
				> How routers share info through routing protocols:
					1) IGPs are globally configured, but enabled on individual interfaces on a router (thru the use of 'network' cmd during protocol config)
					2) > IGP config: R1(config-router)#network <networkID> <wildcardMask>
					   > network cmd: routing protocol will be enabled on all interfaces of R1 with IPs falling in specified network range
					   > wildcardMask = 255.255.255.255 - subnetMask
					3) If routing protocol is enabled on an interface, it looks for other device on the link for also running the routing protocol
					4) It does that by sending out and listening for hello packets
					5) If a matching peer is found, an adjecency is formed
					6) Routers then exchnage routing information
				> Configuring an interface as PASSIVE for a routing protocol, allows it to only recieve routing info, but not send routing info out the interface
				> Used for security purpose, when interface is connected to external network => recieves external rouitng info, but doesnt share internal info
				> Best practice to configure loopback interfaces as passive interfaces
			> Types (Detailed):
				1) RIP:
					> Distance Vector Routing Protocol
					> Metric: Hop Count (max. 15) => small/test environments only
					> Network cmd doesn't allow wildcard mask during config (only classful network IP allowed)
					> Auto-summarisation of routes to classful bounds (undesirable) (can be turned off), manual-summarisation of routes possible
					> Default route injection possible
					> RIPv2 > RIPv1(legacy) -> supports VSLM, uses multicast, supports authentication
				2) EIGRP:
					> Distance Vector Routing Protocol
					> Supports large networks, fast convergence time, multicast updates
					> Supports EQMP LB for upto 4 paths (can be incrreased to 32), UEQMP LB configurable
					> Network cmd allows wildcard mask (if no wildcard mask specified, defaults to class boundary)
					> No auto-summarisation by default
					> Autonomus System no.:
						> Multiple eigrp configs can exist in single env
						> Each eigrp config has an AS no. (specified during config)
						> Routers with eigrp configs with same AS no. peer with each other
						> AS no. helps in domain splitting in single env
					> EIGRP Router Id:
						> An IP address used to identify router in eigrp protocol
						> EIGRP Router Id = Highest Loopback Interface IP (if no loopback, Highest Interface IP)
						> Can be maually configured
				3) OSPF:
					> Link State Routing Protocol: Floods the "state" of all links to all others in topology to build a consistent network map across all links
					> Supports large network, fast convergence, multicast updates, open standard, uses Dijkstra's SPF algo for finding best/shortest path
					> OSPF Operations:
						1) Discover neighbour
						2) Form Adjecency with neighbour
						3) Flood Link State Database (LSDB)
						4) Compute Stortest Path
						5) Install best route in routing table
						6) Respond to network changes
					> OSPF Packet types:
						1) Hello: router sends & listens for hello packets on an ospf enabled interface, & forms adjecencies w other routers on the link
						2) DBD (DB Descriptor): Adjecent routers share summary of their LSDB
						3) LSR (Link State Request): If a router is missing info about any of the networks in the recieved DBD, it send its neighbour an LSR
						4) LSA (Link State Advertisement): A routing update, Not a packet type, Contained inside an LSU
						5) LSU (Link State Update): Contains a list of LSAs that should be updated, used during flooding
						6) LSAck (Link State Acknowledgement): Sent to acknowledge an LSA
					> Network cmd mandates using a wildcard mask (no default to class boundary)
					> No auto-summarisation by default, summarisation set through areas
					> Process Id:
						> Multiple Instances of OSPF, each with a different PID, can be configured on a router
						> Different instances of OSPF have differnt Link State Databases
						> Different interfaces on a router can run in different instances of OSPF => DOMAIN SPLITTING
						> PID is locally significant to a router => PID doesnt have to match on neighbour to form adjecency
					> OSPF Router Id:
						> An IP address used to identify router in ospf protocol
						> OSPF Router Id = Highest Loopback Interface IP (if no loopback, Highest Interface IP)
						> Can be manually configured
					> OSPF Areas:
						> Link State Protocol: Every link in network learns about every other link => SLOW & HIGH RESOURCE USAGE on each reconvergence
						> OSPF's Heirarchical Design: large netwok segmented into smaller areas
							> Each link has info for all routes within own area
							> Each link has info for only summarised routes outside own area (=> saves resources and time on reconvergence)
						> OSPF Heirarchy:
							1) For small networks: all routers in area 0
							2) For large networks: 2 level Heirarchichy:
								1) Transit Area (backbone area/area 0):
									> Top of heirarchichy | All traffic transits through transit area | Does not contain end-users
								2) Regular Areas (non-backbone areas):
									> Used to connect end-users to the transit area
						> The area is configured at the interface level with the "network" cmd: R1(config-router)#network 10.0.0.0 0.0.255.255 area 0
						> Area number must match on 2 neighbours for both to in same area
						> Routers need to be in the same area to achieve full adjecency
						> Types of Routers in OSPF:
							1) Backbone Routers:
								> Routers in backbone area
								> Form full adjecency with other backbone routers
								> Maintain full LSDB about all oruters in backbone area
							2) ABRs (Area Border Routers):
								> Routers that have interfaces in different areas (ideally, only 2 areas (Transit & Other), max.3 areas)
								> Functions:
									> They separate LSA flooding zones
									> They become the primary point of address summarisation
									> They function as the source of default routes
									> They maintain the LSDB for each area they are connected to
								> Route Summarisation:
									> ABRs don't automatically summarise routes => unsummarised routes flooded outside areas as well
									> Manual Summarisation to be configured at ABRs
									> At router-protocol config, specify the network range for each area (similar to network cmd)
										R1(config-router)#area 0 10.1.0.0 255.255.0.0
									> A route to another area is shown as O IA (OSPF Inter-Area Route) in the routing table
							3) Normal Internal Routers:
								> Router with all its interfaces in a normal area
								> Maintain a full LSDB of all routers in own area
								> Learn the inter-area routes to other areas through their ABRs
							4) ASBRs (Autonomous System Boundary Routers):
								> At OSPF System Border
								> Redistributes routes from another source (static or other routing protocol) into OSPF
								> Routes redistributed into OSPF show as O E2 (OSPF External Type 2 Routes) in the routing table
					> OSPF Areas Summary:
						> In single area network:
							> Routers learn paths to each destiantion => least cost path to ech destination goes into the routing table
						> In multiple area network:
							> Routers learn paths to each destination in own area => least cost path to each destination in RT
							> Routers learn summary paths to each outside area (going via an ABR) => least cost path to each outside area in RT
					> Speed vs ClockRate vs Bandwidth:
						1) Speed:
							> Speed: The rate at which ethernet interfaces PHYSICALLY tramsit
							> Cmd: R1(config-if)#speed <megabits/s>
							> Default: FastEthernet=100megabits/s | GigabitEthernet=1000megabits/s
							> Must match on both side of the link for link to be up
						2) ClockRate:
							> ClockRate: The rate at which serial interfaces PHYSICALLY transmit
							> Cmd: R1(config-if)#clock rate <kilobits/s>
							> Default: SerialInterfaces=1.544mbps
							> Must match on both side of the link for link to be up
						3) Bandwidth:
							> Bandwidth: A software reference to the speed/clockrate of an interface
							> Used by software policies for cost calculation of a path by routing protocols, & QOS policy
							> Ideally & Default, Interface Bandwidth = Interface Speed/Clockrate
							> 'bandwidth' cmd => manupulate software policy => change cost of a path in OSPF
							> Cmd: R1(config-if)#bandwidth <megabits/s>
							> Differnet bandwidth on end of link => asymmetric application of software policy 
					> OSPF Cost Metric Calculation:
						> Shortest Path First Algorithm:
							> SPF: Used to calculates total cost of each path to each destination
							> Total Cost = Cumulative cost of all outgoing interfaces
						> Cost Metric:
							> Automatically derived from INTERFACE BANDWIDTH
							> Cost = ceil(Reference Bandwidth/Outgoing Interface Bandwidth)
							> Reference Bandwidth:
								> Default: 100 mbps => Cost of all interfaces faster than 100mbps=1 => undesirable => manually set ref bandwidth
								> Eg: Cost_FE=100/100=1 | Cost_T1=100/1.544=64
								> Manual Set Ref Bandwidth: at router config level -> auto-cost refernce-bandwidth <mbps>
									> Reference bandwidth must be changed on all routers
						> Manupulating OSPF Path <= Manupulating OSPF Path Cost <= 2 ways:
							> Manually Changing Interface OSPF Cost (recommended)
							> Manually Changing Interface Bandwidth (affects other software policies eg. QOS)
						> Different interface costs on ends of the link => asymmetric routing
					> OSPF Packet:
						> OSPF Packet Header: IP/Layer3 header of any packet has protocol field (8 bits) - OSPF:89 TCP:6 UDP:17 (most common tcp & udp)
						> OSPF Packet Data:
							> Version: OSPFv2 or OSPFv3
							> Type: 1-Hello 2-DBD 3-LSR 4-LSU 5-LSAck
							> Router Id (of advertising router)
							> Area Id (of interface of advertising router)
							> Checksum (for corruption check)
							> Authentication Type: 0-No 1-PlainText 2-MD5
							> Authentication
							> Data: Payload data according to type
						> OSPF Hello Packet:
							> Routers discover each other & form adjececny via hello packets
							> Routers send hello packets out each ospf enabled interface (except passive interfaces)
							> Multicast to 224.0.0.5 ('all ospf routers')
							> Sent every 10s (default)
							> OSPF Hello Packet Data Data:
								> Router Id (32 bit)
								> Hello Interval: Frequency of sending hello packet (default: 10s)
								> Dead Interval: how long the neighbours should wait before declaring it as down (default: 4xHelloInterval)
								> Neighbours: List of adjececnt ospf routers it has recieved hello packets from
								> Area Id: Area no. configured for the interface
								> Router Priority: to select DR & BDR
								> DR & BDR IPv4 address (if known)
								> Authentication Flag: Authentication details, if configured
								> Stub Area Flag: If the area is a stub area, stub areas have a default route to ABR instead of learning IA routes
					> OSPF Adjecency:
						> These settings must match for a pair of routers to form adjecency with each other:
							> Must be in each other's neighbours list
							> Hello & Dead intervals
							> Area Id
							> IP subnet
							> Authentication flag
							> Stub area flag
						> On MTU mismatch b/w 2 ospf routers, they will form adjececncy till EXCHANGE state only (i.e. wont share routes with each other)
							> MTU: Maximum Transmission Unit: Maximum size of a packet
							> MTU is configured at interface level (1500 bytes default) - interface MTU(all packets) or interface ip MTU(ip packets)
						> Adjececncy forming process:
							1) 1st router sends a hello packet with its router-id multicat to 225.0.0.5, empty neighbour list initially
							2) Neighbour see this packet, replies with unicast hello packet with its router-id & 1st router in N-list
							3) 1st router recieves this, replies with hello packet unicast, with 2nd router in N-list
							4) 2-way state achieved/Neighbours discovered (3 hello paackets shared)
							5) Then both negotiate who is going to send info first (higher router id shares first)
							6) They share DBD (summary of their LSDB), with each DBD the other acknowledges with an LSAck
							7) Routers share with each other LSR for only important/required routes, other router replies with an LSU
							8) Both finally acknowledge each other with an LSAck, telling they have recieved all required information
						> Neighbour states through adjecency forming process:
							1) Down: No active neighbour detected
							2) Init: Hello packet recieved from neighbour
							3) 2-way: Own router-id recieved in hello packet
							4) Exstart: Primary & secondary roles determined
							5) Exchange: DBDs sent
							6) Loading: Exchange of LSRs & LSUs
							7) Full: Neighbours fully adjececnt
					> OSPF on Point to point vs Multiaccess Segments:
						1) Point to point links (2 nodes/routers directly connected to each other, through serial interfaces):
							> OSPF router pairs form a FULL adjececncy
							> NO DR-BDR election
						2) Multiaccess segments (>2 routers inter-connected via switch, through ethernet interfaces to form single shared network/LAN):
							> If all routers form FULL adjececncy w each other => repetetive info would be shared to the same link => INEFFICIENT!
							> DR (Designated Router) & BDR (Backup Designated Router):
								> Router pair is elected as DR-BDR, they acquire routing info from all routers in LAN, consolidate & spread it around
								> Router with highest priority = DR | Router with 2nd highest priority = BDR
								> Priority: 0(lowest)...1(default)...255(highest) | If priority tie, highest router-id is used
								> DR/BDR election is done when all routers in LAN reach 2-WAY state
									> After DR-BDR election, other routers reach FULL state with the DR & BDR
									> Other routers remain in 2-WAY state with each other
									> DR & BDR reach FULL state with each other
								> DR/BDR election is done on the interface-level of a router
								> Reconvergence:
									> If a link's state changes, it sends a multicast LSU to 224.0.0.6 (all DRs) (BDR learns as well)
									> DR (only DR, not BDR) multicasts the update to 224.0.0.5 (all ospf routers)

Routing Database
	All routes are added to their corresponding routing databases first, before making it into the routing table
	Separate database for each type of route - connected routes db, static routes db, dynamic routes db for each protocol
	All routes across all databases are compared, best route for each destination makes it into the routing table


------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ROUTING_LAB--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
---------FUNDAMENTALS------------
Adding IP Address on Router's Interface
	R1(config)#interface <intNo>			// eg. intNo = FastEthernet0/0
	R1(config-if)#ip address <ipAddress> <subnetMask>

Show Routing Table
	R1#show ip route

Adding Static Route
	R1(config)#ip route <destinationNetIPAddr> <destinationNetMask> <nextHopIPAddr>

Adding Floating Static Route
	R1(config)#ip route <destinationNetIPAddr> <destinationNetMask> <nextHopIPAddr> <AD>

Creating Loopback Interface
	R1(config)#interface loopback <loopbackIntNo>	// loopbackIntNo = 0,1,...
	R1(config-if)#no shutdown			// to bring interface up
	R1(config-if)#ip address <IPAddr> <netMask>	// add ip addr to int

Show Active Routing Protocols on Router
	R1#show ip protocols

Configure Passive Interface
	R1(config-router)#passive-interface <intNo>	// eg.intNo=f2/0,loopback 0|at protocol config
			OR
	R1(config-router)#passive-interface default
	R1(config-router)#no passive-interface <intNo>
	R1(config-router)#no passive-interface <intNo>
			.
			.
			.

Default Route Injection
	// To add a default route route to edge router, let protocol propogate it to all routers
	R4(config)#ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 <nextHopIP>	// configure default static route
	R4(config)#router <protocol>			// enter protocol configuration prompt
	R4(config-router)#default-information originate	// protocl spreads default route from R4 to all

------------RIP-------------
RIP Config
	R1(config)#router rip		// 'router' cmd for configuring routing protocols
	R1(config-router)#version 2	// 'version 2' to use RIPv2
	R1(config-router)#network 10.0.0.0// rip enabled on int w IP in 10.0.0.0, default classful mask
	R1(config-router)#no auto-summary// disable auto-summarisation

RIP Manual Summarisation of Routes
				  f1/0 10.0.0.1/24
				  /
		10.1.0.1/24 f0/0-R1-f2/0 10.0.1.1/24
				 \
				 f3/0 10.0.2.1/24
	R1(config)#interface f0/0
	R1(config-if)#ip summary-address rip 10.0.0.0 255.255.0.0

RIP Verification
	1. R1#show ip protocols			// inner protocol details for all configured protocols
	2. R1#show runnig-config | section rip	// active rip config in ram
	3. R1#show ip route			// rip routes in routing table
	4. R1#show ip rip database		// rip database

----------EIGRP--------------
EIGRP Config
	R1(config)#router eigrp <ASno>				// r w same eigrp AS no. peer w e o
	R1(config-router)#network <networkId> <wildcardMask>	// enable eigrp on int w IP in netwokId

EIGRP Verification
	1. R1#show ip protocols
	2. R1#show running-config | section eigrp
	3. R1#show ip route
	4. R1#show ip eigrp database
	5. R1#show ip eigrp interfaces		// interface related eigrp details, for all interfaces
	6. R1#show ip eigrp neighbours		// neighbour related eigrp details, for all neighbours

EIGRP Manual Router Id Config
	R1(config-router)#eigrp router-id <routerIdIP>
	R1(config)#clear ip eigrp process	// routerId resets on process reload(or reconfig eigrp)

------------OSPF---------------
OSPF Config
	R1(config)#router ospf <processId>	// pid is locally significant, is for domain splitting
	R1(config-router)#network <networkId> <wildcardMask> area <areaNo> // areaNo for summarisation

OSPF Verification
	1. R1#show running-config | section ospf// protocol config verification
	2. R1#show ip protocols			// protocol details verification
	3. R1#show ip ospf interface [<intNo>|brief]	// interfaces verification
	4. R1#show ip ospf neighbour		// adjecency/neighbours verification
	5. R1#show ip ospf database		// routing database verification
	6. R1#show ip route			// routing table verification
	7. R1#debug ip ospf adjecency		// debug ospf adjecency formation
	8. R1#debug ip ospf hello		// debug hello sending & recieving process

OSPF Manual Router Id Config
	R1(config-router)#router-id <routerIdIP>
	R1(config)#clear ip ospf process	// router id sets on process reload (or reconfig ospf)

OSPF Manual Route Summarisation through Areas on ABRs
	R1(config-router)#area <areaNo> range <networkIP> <subnetMask>
		// routes within this area will be summarised to this when shared outside area

OSPF Manual Set Reference Bandwidth
	R1(config-router)#auto-cost reference-bandwidth <mbps>	// must be set on all routers in domain

OSPF Manual Set Interface OSPF Cost
	R1(config-if)#ip ospf cost <cost>// must be same on both sides of link for symmetric routing

OSPF Manual Set Hello Interval
	R1(config-if)#ip ospf hello-interval <seconds>	// must match on ends of link for adj formation

OSPF Manual Set Priority (for DR/BDR election)
	R1(config-if)#ip ospf priority <number0-255>	// restart OSPF on interface => flap interface